.docname {Authoring Workflows}
.docauthor {BakingRL}
.include {docs}
.doclang {en}

This page shows the common ways to author a BakingRL plugin package after it
has been generated.

The generated template uses Vite, TypeScript, and ESM entry files. Each export
listed in `bakingrl.plugin.json` must have a matching Vite build input that
emits the declared `dist/...js` file.

## Classic Generated Package

Create a package, install dependencies, and build it:

```sh
create-bakingrl-plugin rl-scoreboard
cd rl-scoreboard
npm install
npm run build
npm run validate
npm run pack
npm run install:local
```

The default `fullstack-plugin` template contains:

```txt
bakingrl.plugin.json
package.json
vite.config.ts
src/visuals/scoreboard/index.ts
src/components/team-badge/index.ts
src/services/match-stats/index.ts
src/connectors/twitch/index.ts
```

Use `npm run add` to add another export without manually editing the manifest
and Vite input block:

```sh
npm run add -- visual boostMeter
npm run add -- component PlayerCard
npm run add -- service matchStats
npm run add -- connector twitch
```

After generation, edit the new source file and rebuild.

## TypeScript Visual

A visual is the most direct export type. It receives a DOM root and host APIs.

`src/visuals/scoreboard/index.ts`:

```ts
import { defineVisual, type VisualContext } from "@bakingrl/plugin-sdk";

export default defineVisual({
  mount(context: VisualContext) {
    context.root.className = "scoreboard";
    context.root.textContent = "Waiting for match data";

    return context.bus.subscribe("UpdateState", (event) => {
      const teams = event.Data.Game.Teams;
      const blue = teams.find((team) => team.TeamNum === 0);
      const orange = teams.find((team) => team.TeamNum === 1);

      context.root.textContent =
        `${blue?.Name ?? "Blue"} ${blue?.Score ?? 0} - ` +
        `${orange?.Score ?? 0} ${orange?.Name ?? "Orange"}`;
    });
  }
});
```

Manifest entry:

```json
{
  "exports": {
    "visuals": {
      "scoreboard": {
        "entry": "dist/visuals/scoreboard.js",
        "defaultSize": [600, 90]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Vite input:

```ts
rollupOptions: {
  input: {
    "visuals/scoreboard": "src/visuals/scoreboard/index.ts"
  }
}
```

The output name `visuals/scoreboard` is what creates
`dist/visuals/scoreboard.js`.

## TypeScript Component

Components are reusable DOM fragments mounted by visuals through the host.

`src/components/team-badge/index.ts`:

```ts
import { defineComponent, type ComponentContext } from "@bakingrl/plugin-sdk";

export default defineComponent({
  mount(context: ComponentContext, props: Record<string, unknown>) {
    const teamName = String(props.teamName ?? "Team");
    const color = String(props.color ?? "#38bdf8");

    context.root.innerHTML = `
      <span class="team-badge">
        <span class="team-badge__dot"></span>
        <span>${teamName}</span>
      </span>
    `;

    context.root
      .querySelector<HTMLElement>(".team-badge__dot")
      ?.style.setProperty("background", color);
  }
});
```

Visuals load components by reference:

```ts
const handle = await context.components.load("com.example.ui/TeamBadge");
const cleanup = await handle.mount(context.root, {
  teamName: "Blue",
  color: "#38bdf8"
});
```

Declare component imports in the consuming package manifest when the component
comes from another package.

## TypeScript Service

Services run in the backend sandbox and expose RPC methods.

`src/services/match-stats/index.ts`:

```ts
import { defineService, type ServiceContext } from "@bakingrl/plugin-sdk";

let lastSnapshot: unknown = null;

export default defineService({
  mount(context: ServiceContext) {
    return context.bus.subscribe("UpdateState", (event) => {
      lastSnapshot = event.Data;
      context.registry.set("plugin.com.example.scoreboard.current", event.Data);
    });
  },
  methods: {
    snapshot() {
      return lastSnapshot;
    },
    reset() {
      lastSnapshot = null;
      return { ok: true };
    }
  }
});
```

Visuals can call a permitted service:

```ts
const snapshot = await context.services.call(
  "com.example.scoreboard/matchStats",
  "snapshot"
);
```

## Separate HTML And CSS Files

Use this pattern when a visual is easier to maintain with separate markup and
style files. Vite can import HTML as raw text and bundle CSS from the entry.

Recommended file layout:

```txt
src/visuals/scoreboard/index.ts
src/visuals/scoreboard/template.html
src/visuals/scoreboard/style.css
```

`template.html`:

```html
<div class="scoreboard">
  <div class="scoreboard__team" data-blue>Blue</div>
  <div class="scoreboard__score" data-score>0 - 0</div>
  <div class="scoreboard__team" data-orange>Orange</div>
</div>
```

`style.css`:

```css
.scoreboard {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 16px;
  height: 100%;
  padding: 0 24px;
  color: white;
  background: rgba(10, 14, 18, 0.86);
  font: 700 24px Inter, Arial, sans-serif;
}

.scoreboard__score {
  color: #38bdf8;
}

.scoreboard__team[data-orange] {
  text-align: right;
}
```

`index.ts`:

```ts
import { defineVisual, type VisualContext } from "@bakingrl/plugin-sdk";
import template from "./template.html?raw";
import "./style.css";

export default defineVisual({
  mount(context: VisualContext) {
    context.root.innerHTML = template;

    const blueName = context.root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-blue]");
    const orangeName = context.root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-orange]");
    const score = context.root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-score]");

    return context.bus.subscribe("UpdateState", (event) => {
      const teams = event.Data.Game.Teams;
      const blue = teams.find((team) => team.TeamNum === 0);
      const orange = teams.find((team) => team.TeamNum === 1);

      if (blueName) blueName.textContent = blue?.Name ?? "Blue";
      if (orangeName) orangeName.textContent = orange?.Name ?? "Orange";
      if (score) score.textContent = `${blue?.Score ?? 0} - ${orange?.Score ?? 0}`;
    });
  }
});
```

The generated template includes `vite/client` TypeScript types, so `?raw`
imports are understood by `npm run check`.

## Static Assets

Place images, fonts, and other package assets under an `assets/` directory and
resolve them through the SDK context:

```ts
const logoUrl = context.assets.url("assets/logo.png");
context.root.innerHTML = `<img src="${logoUrl}" alt="">`;
```

Do not build absolute local filesystem paths into plugin code. The host decides
how package files are served.

## Settings

Visual exports can declare instance settings in the manifest:

```json
{
  "exports": {
    "visuals": {
      "scoreboard": {
        "entry": "dist/visuals/scoreboard.js",
        "defaultSize": [600, 90],
        "settings": "src/visuals/scoreboard/settings.schema.json"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Read settings at runtime with the `setting` helper:

```ts
import { defineVisual, setting, type VisualContext } from "@bakingrl/plugin-sdk";

type Instance = {
  accent: string;
};

const instances = new Map<HTMLElement, Instance>();

function render(context: VisualContext, instance: Instance) {
  context.root.style.setProperty("--accent", instance.accent);
}

export default defineVisual({
  mount(context: VisualContext) {
    const instance = {
      accent: setting(context.settings, "accent", "#38bdf8")
    };
    instances.set(context.root, instance);
    render(context, instance);
    return () => instances.delete(context.root);
  },
  update(context) {
    const instance = instances.get(context.root);
    if (!instance) return;
    instance.accent = setting(context.settings, "accent", "#38bdf8");
    render(context, instance);
  }
});
```

When an instance setting affects the rendered output, implement
`update(context)` and rerender from the new `context.settings`. The editor
pushes settings and metadata changes to mounted visuals. A host may remount
visuals that do not expose `update`, but that is a fallback behavior, not the
recommended refresh path. Relying on remounting can reset local state,
subscriptions, timers, and animations.

Packages can also declare package-level settings:

```json
{
  "settings": "src/settings.schema.json"
}
```

Package-level secret fields use `x-bakingrl-secret: true`, are stored in the OS
keychain, and are readable only from connectors through `context.secrets`.
Non-secret package settings are available through `context.settings`.
Use `context.secrets.configured(key)` for UI/status checks; call
`context.secrets.get(key)` only when the connector actually needs the value.
The host caches secret values in memory after the first keychain read.

## Visual Sizing

The page or layout editor owns the outer rectangle of each visual. Your visual
should render inside `context.root` and treat that element as the sizing
boundary. `defaultSize` in the manifest is only the initial placement size; the
user can resize the instance afterward.

For flexible widgets, use normal CSS with `width: 100%`, `height: 100%`, and
responsive layout inside the root. For fixed-design broadcast graphics, wrap the
design in an inner element with a known design size and scale that inner element
to fit `context.root` with a `ResizeObserver`. Return a cleanup function that
disconnects the observer.

Do not position visual content relative to the viewport, mutate DOM outside
`context.root`, or depend on editor reloads to apply sizing or settings
changes.

## Package Configuration Page

A package can declare one private configuration page. This page opens from the
package configuration action inside BakingRL. It is not a public page template
and its private visuals are not shown in the normal visual picker.

```json
{
  "exports": {
    "configuration": {
      "path": "pages/configuration.json",
      "title": "Package Settings",
      "visuals": {
        "settingsPanel": {
          "entry": "dist/configuration/settings-panel.js",
          "defaultSize": [1200, 740]
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The host renders every package configuration page on a fixed `1200x740`
canvas. Design configuration visuals for that size and keep persistent package
state behind host APIs or package settings instead of local filesystem paths.
If the package declares secrets, BakingRL renders those fields in host-owned UI
next to the custom page so plugin frontend code never sees submitted secret
values.

## Local Test Fixtures

The SDK mock helpers let simple tests mount exports without the BakingRL host:

```ts
import { createMockVisualContext } from "@bakingrl/plugin-sdk";
import visual from "../src/visuals/scoreboard";

const context = createMockVisualContext();
const cleanup = await visual.mount(context);

if (typeof cleanup === "function") cleanup();
```

Telemetry helpers create typed frames for tests around your own rendering
functions:

```ts
import { mockUpdateState } from "@bakingrl/plugin-sdk";

const frame = mockUpdateState({
  TimeSeconds: 120
});
```

For full runtime behavior, build the package, run `npm run install:local`, and
reload packages in the BakingRL app.
